1
00:00:00,667 --> 00:00:03,607
In eighteen thirty-two, Joseph Henry,

2
00:00:03,627 --> 00:00:06,407
the man who built the great electromagnets,

3
00:00:06,427 --> 00:00:09,207
noticed something odd on his bench.

4
00:00:09,227 --> 00:00:13,127
He had a small battery, far too weak to give a shock.

5
00:00:13,147 --> 00:00:16,647
When he connected it to a short wire and broke the circuit,

6
00:00:16,667 --> 00:00:18,087
nothing happened.

7
00:00:18,107 --> 00:00:21,687
But when the wire was long, and wound into a coil,

8
00:00:21,707 --> 00:00:24,807
breaking the circuit made a bright spark jump across the

9
00:00:24,827 --> 00:00:25,767
gap.

10
00:00:25,787 --> 00:00:27,567
The battery had not changed.

11
00:00:27,587 --> 00:00:30,337
The spark was coming from the coil.

12
00:00:30,427 --> 00:00:33,207
Something in that coil was refusing to let the current

13
00:00:33,227 --> 00:00:34,287
stop.

14
00:00:34,307 --> 00:00:36,727
That something is inductance.

15
00:00:36,747 --> 00:00:39,767
It is in every motor, every transformer,

16
00:00:39,787 --> 00:00:42,657
and every power line you will ever work on.

17
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,860
Last time, we saw that a current makes a magnetic field,

18
00:00:47,880 --> 00:00:50,270
and that a coil concentrates it.

19
00:00:50,480 --> 00:00:54,030
Now look more closely at the moment the current starts.

20
00:00:54,160 --> 00:00:57,740
The field is not there, and then it is.

21
00:00:57,760 --> 00:00:59,500
It has to be built.

22
00:00:59,520 --> 00:01:03,020
And like the electric field between the plates of a capacitor,

23
00:01:03,040 --> 00:01:05,820
a magnetic field holds energy.

24
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:08,420
That energy has to come from somewhere.

25
00:01:08,440 --> 00:01:10,460
It comes from the circuit.

26
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:12,540
So while the field is being built,

27
00:01:12,560 --> 00:01:14,980
the circuit has to do extra work,

28
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:17,580
on top of whatever it does to push current through the

29
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:18,740
wire.

30
00:01:18,760 --> 00:01:21,620
Once the current is steady, the field is steady,

31
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:24,140
and it costs nothing more to keep.

32
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:27,860
The energy sits there, stored in the space in and around

33
00:01:27,880 --> 00:01:28,790
the coil.

34
00:01:30,133 --> 00:01:32,683
How does the circuit feel that extra work?

35
00:01:33,093 --> 00:01:34,283
Through a voltage.

36
00:01:34,613 --> 00:01:38,353
In eighteen thirty-one, Michael Faraday found that a changing

37
00:01:38,373 --> 00:01:42,473
magnetic field produces a voltage in any wire it passes

38
00:01:42,493 --> 00:01:42,843
through.

39
00:01:43,253 --> 00:01:45,963
We will give that discovery a video of its own.

40
00:01:46,053 --> 00:01:48,363
Here we need only one consequence.

41
00:01:48,693 --> 00:01:51,163
A coil sits in its own field.

42
00:01:51,253 --> 00:01:54,833
So when its current changes, its own field changes,

43
00:01:54,853 --> 00:01:58,043
and a voltage appears across the coil itself.

44
00:01:58,213 --> 00:02:00,123
Which way does that voltage point?

45
00:02:00,453 --> 00:02:03,953
In eighteen thirty-four, the physicist Heinrich Lenz gave

46
00:02:03,973 --> 00:02:04,523
the rule.

47
00:02:04,933 --> 00:02:08,163
It always opposes the change that caused it.

48
00:02:08,293 --> 00:02:11,513
Try to raise the current, and the coil pushes back against

49
00:02:11,533 --> 00:02:12,433
the rise.

50
00:02:12,453 --> 00:02:15,873
Try to reduce the current, and the coil pushes it forward,

51
00:02:15,893 --> 00:02:17,473
trying to keep it going.

52
00:02:17,493 --> 00:02:19,793
A coil does not resist current.

53
00:02:19,813 --> 00:02:22,043
It resists a change in current.

54
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:25,230
Think of a heavy flywheel.

55
00:02:25,560 --> 00:02:28,700
It is hard to get turning, and once it is turning,

56
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:30,030
it is hard to stop.

57
00:02:30,440 --> 00:02:32,190
It does not object to spinning.

58
00:02:32,440 --> 00:02:34,990
It objects to changing its speed.

59
00:02:35,160 --> 00:02:37,830
A coil treats current the same way.

60
00:02:37,960 --> 00:02:40,990
How strongly it objects is its inductance.

61
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:43,700
The faster you try to change the current,

62
00:02:43,720 --> 00:02:46,270
the larger the voltage the coil produces.

63
00:02:46,680 --> 00:02:49,540
The ratio between the two is the inductance,

64
00:02:49,560 --> 00:02:50,430
written L.

65
00:02:50,920 --> 00:02:54,260
The voltage across a coil equals its inductance,

66
00:02:54,280 --> 00:02:57,030
times the rate at which the current is changing.

67
00:02:57,160 --> 00:03:00,740
The word itself was coined in eighteen eighty-six by Oliver

68
00:03:00,760 --> 00:03:03,790
Heaviside, a self-taught English engineer.

69
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:06,350
The unit came seven years later.

70
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:09,460
At the International Electrical Congress in Chicago,

71
00:03:09,480 --> 00:03:12,820
in eighteen ninety-three, it was named the henry,

72
00:03:12,840 --> 00:03:15,270
after the man who saw the spark.

73
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:19,860
One henry means this: if the current changes by one ampere

74
00:03:19,880 --> 00:03:22,990
each second, the coil produces one volt.

75
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:24,830
It is a large unit.

76
00:03:25,080 --> 00:03:28,260
The inductors on a circuit board are measured in thousandths

77
00:03:28,280 --> 00:03:30,110
or millionths of a henry.

78
00:03:31,467 --> 00:03:34,057
What decides the inductance of a coil?

79
00:03:34,187 --> 00:03:37,217
Only its shape, and what it is wound on.

80
00:03:37,627 --> 00:03:39,697
Start with the number of turns.

81
00:03:39,867 --> 00:03:42,967
Double the turns, and the same current makes twice the

82
00:03:42,987 --> 00:03:43,537
field.

83
00:03:43,947 --> 00:03:47,487
But that field now passes through twice as many turns,

84
00:03:47,507 --> 00:03:50,017
and each of them adds its own voltage.

85
00:03:50,347 --> 00:03:52,577
So the inductance does not double.

86
00:03:52,747 --> 00:03:54,697
It goes up four times.

87
00:03:54,827 --> 00:03:58,217
Inductance grows with the square of the number of turns.

88
00:03:58,347 --> 00:04:01,127
A wider coil encloses more field,

89
00:04:01,147 --> 00:04:03,537
so inductance grows with the area.

90
00:04:03,867 --> 00:04:06,967
A longer coil, with the same turns spread out,

91
00:04:06,987 --> 00:04:10,977
has a weaker field, so inductance falls with the length.

92
00:04:11,067 --> 00:04:12,737
And then there is the core.

93
00:04:13,067 --> 00:04:16,727
Put iron inside, and its domains multiply the field,

94
00:04:16,747 --> 00:04:18,817
hundreds or thousands of times.

95
00:04:19,067 --> 00:04:21,617
The inductance is multiplied with it.

96
00:04:21,787 --> 00:04:23,057
Notice one more thing.

97
00:04:23,387 --> 00:04:26,537
Any wire carrying current has a field around it.

98
00:04:26,587 --> 00:04:29,407
So every wire has some inductance,

99
00:04:29,427 --> 00:04:30,777
even a straight one.

100
00:04:30,907 --> 00:04:32,417
Usually it is tiny.

101
00:04:32,667 --> 00:04:35,767
At high frequencies, or on a long power line,

102
00:04:35,787 --> 00:04:37,697
it matters a great deal.

103
00:04:39,067 --> 00:04:41,297
We said the field holds energy.

104
00:04:41,547 --> 00:04:42,457
How much?

105
00:04:42,587 --> 00:04:45,527
The work is done against the coil's voltage while the

106
00:04:45,547 --> 00:04:47,137
current climbs from zero.

107
00:04:47,467 --> 00:04:50,817
Add it all up, and the energy stored is one half,

108
00:04:50,907 --> 00:04:53,977
times the inductance, times the current squared.

109
00:04:54,107 --> 00:04:56,057
Set that beside the capacitor.

110
00:04:56,187 --> 00:04:59,447
There, the energy was one half C V squared,

111
00:04:59,467 --> 00:05:01,137
stored in an electric field.

112
00:05:01,387 --> 00:05:04,247
Here, it is one half L I squared,

113
00:05:04,267 --> 00:05:06,017
stored in a magnetic field.

114
00:05:06,347 --> 00:05:09,057
A capacitor stores energy in voltage.

115
00:05:09,227 --> 00:05:11,577
An inductor stores it in current.

116
00:05:11,627 --> 00:05:14,257
And now Henry's spark makes sense.

117
00:05:14,347 --> 00:05:17,177
Open the switch, and the current must stop.

118
00:05:17,227 --> 00:05:20,887
The field collapses, and all its energy has to go somewhere,

119
00:05:20,907 --> 00:05:21,897
at once.

120
00:05:21,947 --> 00:05:24,967
The coil produces whatever voltage it takes to keep the

121
00:05:24,987 --> 00:05:28,327
current flowing, hundreds or thousands of volts,

122
00:05:28,347 --> 00:05:31,127
enough to drive it straight across the air in the open

123
00:05:31,147 --> 00:05:32,017
switch.

124
00:05:33,400 --> 00:05:36,660
Connect a coil and a resistor to a battery.

125
00:05:36,680 --> 00:05:39,460
The current cannot jump to its final value,

126
00:05:39,480 --> 00:05:42,620
because the coil will not allow a sudden change.

127
00:05:42,640 --> 00:05:46,660
It climbs, quickly at first, then more slowly.

128
00:05:46,680 --> 00:05:50,500
The pace is set by one number: the inductance divided

129
00:05:50,520 --> 00:05:52,110
by the resistance.

130
00:05:52,160 --> 00:05:54,190
This is the time constant.

131
00:05:54,240 --> 00:05:57,300
After one time constant the current has reached about

132
00:05:57,320 --> 00:05:59,540
two thirds of its final value.

133
00:05:59,560 --> 00:06:03,310
After five, it is there for every practical purpose.

134
00:06:03,480 --> 00:06:06,950
Once the current is steady, the coil does nothing at all.

135
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:08,870
It is just a piece of wire.

136
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:12,100
But an alternating current is always changing,

137
00:06:12,120 --> 00:06:14,820
so the coil opposes it all the time,

138
00:06:14,840 --> 00:06:18,190
and the faster it alternates, the stronger the opposition.

139
00:06:18,600 --> 00:06:23,060
So an inductor passes direct current and holds back alternating

140
00:06:23,080 --> 00:06:23,630
current.

141
00:06:23,880 --> 00:06:26,510
That is exactly the opposite of a capacitor.

142
00:06:26,760 --> 00:06:30,500
The two are mirror images, and much of electrical engineering

143
00:06:30,520 --> 00:06:33,310
is built on playing one against the other.

144
00:06:34,667 --> 00:06:37,537
Engineers soon learned to use the spark.

145
00:06:37,627 --> 00:06:41,687
In eighteen fifty-one, Heinrich Ruhmkorff in Paris built

146
00:06:41,707 --> 00:06:45,127
induction coils that turned a few volts from a battery

147
00:06:45,147 --> 00:06:48,087
into sparks many centimetres long.

148
00:06:48,107 --> 00:06:51,807
The same idea fires the spark plugs in a petrol engine.

149
00:06:51,827 --> 00:06:54,207
They also learned to respect it.

150
00:06:54,227 --> 00:06:56,674
Switch off a relay or a motor carelessly,

151
00:06:56,694 --> 00:06:59,767
and the voltage from its coil can destroy the switch,

152
00:06:59,787 --> 00:07:01,927
or the transistor that drives it.

153
00:07:01,947 --> 00:07:05,207
That is why you will see a diode connected across almost

154
00:07:05,227 --> 00:07:08,967
every relay coil: it gives the current a safe path while

155
00:07:08,987 --> 00:07:10,527
the field dies away.

156
00:07:10,547 --> 00:07:14,087
In a power supply, an inductor called a choke smooths

157
00:07:14,107 --> 00:07:17,527
the current, just as a capacitor smooths the voltage.

158
00:07:17,547 --> 00:07:21,047
In eighteen ninety-nine, Michael Pupin added coils at

159
00:07:21,067 --> 00:07:23,047
intervals along telephone lines,

160
00:07:23,067 --> 00:07:25,407
and made long-distance calls possible.

161
00:07:25,427 --> 00:07:28,647
And on the grid, the inductance of the lines and windings

162
00:07:28,667 --> 00:07:31,487
themselves decides how power flows,

163
00:07:31,507 --> 00:07:34,967
how far the voltage drops, and how large a fault current

164
00:07:34,987 --> 00:07:35,937
can become.

165
00:07:37,300 --> 00:07:39,130
So let's put it together.

166
00:07:39,220 --> 00:07:42,000
A magnetic field takes energy to build,

167
00:07:42,020 --> 00:07:44,170
and gives it back when it collapses.

168
00:07:44,500 --> 00:07:48,250
So a coil opposes any change in its own current.

169
00:07:48,500 --> 00:07:52,010
That property is inductance, measured in henries.

170
00:07:52,180 --> 00:07:55,120
It grows with the square of the turns and the area of

171
00:07:55,140 --> 00:07:58,490
the coil, and it is multiplied by an iron core.

172
00:07:58,740 --> 00:08:00,960
The energy is stored in the field,

173
00:08:00,980 --> 00:08:03,850
and it equals one half L I squared.

174
00:08:04,100 --> 00:08:06,970
An inductor takes time to carry a current,

175
00:08:07,020 --> 00:08:10,490
passes a steady one, and holds back a changing one.

176
00:08:10,580 --> 00:08:13,280
Twice now we have leaned on one fact:

177
00:08:13,300 --> 00:08:16,170
a changing magnetic field makes a voltage.

178
00:08:16,500 --> 00:08:19,840
Next time, we look at how the electric field and the magnetic

179
00:08:19,860 --> 00:08:21,290
field are tied together.

180
00:08:21,540 --> 00:08:25,040
If this helped you see it, and not just calculate it,

181
00:08:25,060 --> 00:08:28,090
please like, share, and subscribe to this channel.
